# WooCommerce POS Coding Conventions

## Services Pattern (Singleton)
```php
class Auth {
    private static $instance = null;
    private function __construct() {}
    public static function instance(): self {
        if ( null === self::$instance ) {
            self::$instance = new self();
        }
        return self::$instance;
    }
}
```

## Logging
Use the Logger class, never `error_log()`:
```php
use WCPOS\WooCommercePOS\Logger;
Logger::log( 'Session revoked for user: ' . $user_id );
```

## Backwards Compatibility
Compatibility is owed to consumers, not to code. The consumers are the REST contract the app reads, hooks and filters Pro or a documented extension uses, public methods Pro calls, and anything a framework invokes for us (an override of a WordPress, WooCommerce or vendored parent method, an interface implementation, a WP-CLI command method, a callback registered by name on a hook, REST route, cron or shortcode). Before changing one, grep where its consumer lives: Free, Pro and `next` for PHP symbols and hooks; the app (`../monorepo`) for a REST route, field, header or option; the `wcpos/extensions` catalog and `docs.wcpos.com` for anything a documented extension hooks. A grep of the plugin repos alone proves nothing about a client-facing field. When a consumer exists, add parameters with defaults, deprecate in `X.Y` naming the consumer in the tag, remove in `X.Y+2` at the earliest:
```php
public function get_user_data( WP_User $user, bool $is_web_frontend = false ): array {}
```
When no consumer exists in any of those places, the symbol is dead code: delete it and its direct test in the same PR. A method's own unit test is not a consumer; test seams the suite relies on (reset helpers, injection setters, unregister helpers) are test tooling and stay. Do not keep it "for callers" nobody can name, do not mark it `@deprecated` instead of deleting it, and do not add a test that it is absent (test the behaviour the replacement provides). Ruled by Paul on 2026-10-20 after #2371; the 1.11.x leftovers are inventoried in `.claude/handoffs/2026-10-20-remove-bc-shims-in-v2/` for removal in v2.

## Common Pitfalls

### 1. Assuming Method Context
Don't assume where a method is called from. Use explicit context parameters rather than baking in assumptions about the caller.

### 2. Not Checking $_REQUEST Origin
Always check context before using `$_REQUEST`. Sanitize with `sanitize_text_field()`.

### 3. Minimize Admin Hook Footprint
Only register hooks on pages where they're needed. `admin_post_{action}` hooks fire on `admin-post.php`, not the originating page — register them via request action check in `admin_init`, not screen-dependent classes.

## Extensions
WCPOS supports installable extensions. The catalog lives at `wcpos/extensions` repo (`catalog.json`). Free plugin provides `GET /wcpos/v1/extensions`; pro plugin adds `POST /wcpos/v1/extensions/action`.
